description: Proposed: `make rows` and the gate reuse a row's result when its inputs have not changed, and run rows in parallel, through the gate's existing machinery.
 
 The last full row runs on the llvm-parity branch took 3.9 to 4.2 hours for 119 to 134 rows, and a pin move makes the last one worthless. Most of that reruns rows whose inputs have not changed, one at a time: `workflow.sh rows` runs each row serially with `LW_ATTEST=0`. `gate.sh` already runs `LW_GATE_J` rows at once and skips a row whose `attest.sh key` matches its last green run, but a phase-gate decision turns that off, because the key sees only what `affected.sh`'s table names.
 
 1. Make the key trustworthy: it also covers the toolchain a row uses (clang and lld, Go, the wazero runner) and the `LW_*` variables it reads, declared in the table. An audit row runs a row in a worktree holding only its declared inputs and requires the same verdict; a row that reads something undeclared fails the audit and is not reused. Gate decisions then reuse audited rows.
 2. `rows` and `baseline` run through `gate.sh`'s scheduler with attestation on, instead of their own serial loop. An `items.tsv` column marks the rows that must run alone (P12.37, the benchmarks rows, any row using `quiet.sh`). `rows` with no list is the rows whose key moved, not `git diff HEAD`, so a commit no longer hides its rows.
 
 The per-backend cells from git-issue:bc33496 are recorded against the same key, and the ledger stays per machine as agreed there. The design is docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-31-rows-reuse-and-parallel-design.md.
 
 Facts that close it: F1, an unchanged tree's full set reports every audited row attested in under a minute; F2, a planted undeclared input fails the audit; F3, P12.37 meets its bound under the scheduler as it does alone.
 
 Open: the column's name, and whether the audit runs in CI or only locally.

## Comments
[2026-11-01T06:07:26Z] Matt Parrett: Yes to both parts, with two conditions and answers to the two open questions.
 
 Part 1 changes what a green gate decision means: a row verified at this head becomes a row verified at a tree with the same declared inputs. That holds only while the audit holds, so the condition is that a reused row says so where the decision is read: the TAP line carries the attestation key's short form and the date of the audit that licensed the reuse, and the evidence note a PR carries holds the same two fields per reused row. A reader of the note can then tell a run from a reuse without the ledger.
 
 The audit tests verdicts, not reads. A row that reads an undeclared input and still returns the same verdict passes the audit, and its key is silently incomplete until the input changes the verdict later. A read trace under the audit (the files the row's processes open, diffed against the table line) would close that gap; worth a line in Risks, and a follow-up row if a planted silent read gets past the verdict audit.
 
 Part 2 as proposed. The second condition: the scheduler's concurrency defaults to the slot pool, but an alone row's bound is measured on a machine the scheduler has emptied, so the scheduler waits for the slot pool to drain, not only for the rows before it in the list, before an alone row starts.
 
 The column: `run` with values `shared` and `alone`, so a third mode has somewhere to go. The audit: local only. The ledger is per machine and CI has none, so CI keeps running rows fresh; the audit's verdict is recorded in the ledger beside the row's key and table line, and a decision reuses a row only when the ledger shows the audit at the current table line.
 
 Facts that close it hold as written. F3 should measure P12.37 both ways in one session, scheduler and alone, so the comparison is on the same machine state.
